---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Field from '../components/Field.astro';

// Transcripts from one real session on the acceptance application the
// hale repository ships (dna/acceptance/chat-server), with the editor on
// scripted models. Same commands, same Journal with a hosted model.
const session = `$ hale dna init .
wrote   vendor/dna (13 file(s) written; hale.lock pins toolchain 0.19.2)
cut     .hale/dna/baseline.topology (schema 1.19, shape 3853f0f14bbf1639, verdict clean)
created dna/purpose.hl
created dna/assembly.hl
created dna_constitution.hl
edited  main.hl (imports, \`genome\` param, \`adopt Project\`, membrane bindings)
seeded  .hale/dna/journal.jsonl (17 event(s))

$ hale dna run . --observe 5
hale dna run: organism chat (pid 1694293) under LOTUS_OBS=1
hale dna run: membrane bound at .hale/dna
hale dna run: iris at http://127.0.0.1:8787/  (l law · 4 review · 5 organism · m membrane)

$ hale dna ask document the chat server in main.hl
task t1 born for intent i1a08c0786c5 [pending]`;

const review = `$ hale dna review m1
review m1 [pending]: apply m1 (application): document the chat server in main.hl?
  needs maintainer · candidate dbbb49f550f3f021f390710803e48f1b3d1593ff
  mutation m1 (application) by editor · disposition under the grant: escalate
  magnitude: novelty 3

source diff (git 4319af28e30e .. dbbb49f550f3):
  @@ -110,3 +110,4 @@ main locus ChatServer {
   fn main() { ChatServer { }; }
  +// documented by the organism: the rooms are the only way to the signer

semantic diff (hale model diff, baseline .. candidate):
  classification: source-only  (shape_hash 8517c3db7499d3b3 -> 8517c3db7499d3b3)
  no semantic differences

evidence (fmt=0 check=0 verify=0 test=0 diff=0 rollback=0):
  step       ok     code  receipt        bytes
  base       yes    0     e607a8e25cfc   26
  check      yes    0     e3b0c44298fc   0
  verify     yes    0     e3b0c44298fc   0
  test       yes    0     184a3407ce31   67
  rollback   yes    0     a06effb044d9   96
  diff       yes    0     0dfce6838a75   70257

decide: hale dna review m1 approve|revise|reject|abstain [--as <you>] [--comment <c>]

$ hale dna review m1 approve --as riley --comment "the rooms stay the only way"
review m1 settled: approve by riley`;

const after = `hale dna run: m1 requests a restart (apply dbbb49f5… fitness guests_greeted +)
hale dna run: organism restarted (pid 1694392) as 8517c3db7499d3b3 build b1ac50c8c3ef
hale dna run: m1 observed healthy for 5s as 8517c3db7499d3b3

$ hale dna status
mutations:  1 (none applies before a human's verdict on the exact candidate)
  m1 [retained] application: document the chat server in main.hl · task t1 · candidate dbbb49f550f3`;

const history = `$ hale dna history m1
   18  task.born              t1      i1a08c0786c5: document the chat server in main.hl
   23  mutation.worktree      m1      opened .hale/dna/worktrees/m1 at 4319af28…
   24  mutation.located       m1      main.hl under read edit fmt check @.hale/dna/worktrees/m1
   27  mutation.candidate     m1      dbbb49f550f3f021f390710803e48f1b3d1593ff
   30  evidence.check         dbbb49… {"step": "check", "ok": true, "code": 0, "output_digest": "e3b0c442…"}
   33  evidence.rollback      dbbb49… {"step": "rollback", "ok": true, …}
   36  mutation.escalate      m1      application candidate dbbb49f5…
   37  review.requested       review:m1  {"question": "apply m1 (application): …", "subject_digest": "dbbb49f5…", …}
   41  review.settled         m1      approve by riley
   44  mutation.applied       m1      dbbb49f550f3f021f390710803e48f1b3d1593ff
   46  expression.restarted   m1      8517c3db7499d3b3 build b1ac50c8c3ef
   47  expression.observed    m1      healthy 8517c3db7499d3b3 up for 5s
   48  pressure.remeasured    m1      task t1 fitness guests_greeted +: healthy up for 5s
   49  mutation.retained      m1      observed healthy as 8517c3db7499d3b3`;

const law = `group editors = { dna::SourceEditor, dna::WorktreeTools };

constitution Project {
    apply_gated:             forbid reaches(genome, effects(genome_apply)) avoiding dna_gate;
    performers_never_apply:  forbid reaches(performers, effects(genome_apply));
    credentials_sealed:      require sealed(all credentials);
    editors_never_commit:    forbid reaches(editors, effects(repo_write));
    editors_never_apply:     forbid reaches(editors, effects(genome_apply));
    editors_never_learn:     forbid reaches(editors, knowledge);
}`;
---
<Base title="DNA: a governed application">
  <Field kind="tooling" />
  <section class="container prose">
    <span class="tag">/dna</span>
    <h1>An application that changes itself, with you at the door.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      <strong>DNA</strong> is the part of a Hale application that decides how
      the application changes: what may be attempted, who reviews it, what
      evidence counts, what is applied, and what is never applied without a
      human. It ships inside the <code>hale</code> binary as ordinary Hale
      source. You attach it to a program you already have, and from then on
      a change is something you <em>ask for</em> and <em>approve</em>, with
      the receipts to show for it.
    </p>

    <h2>The loop</h2>
    <ol class="loop">
      <li><strong>Ask.</strong> <code>hale dna ask "…"</code> hands the
        organism an intent through its membrane. A durable Task is born.</li>
      <li><strong>Propose.</strong> An editing Attempt works in its own git
        worktree with a grant of exactly four tools: read, edit, format,
        check. Anything more is a build failure, not a runtime rule.</li>
      <li><strong>Verify.</strong> The candidate is formatted, checked,
        verified, tested, semantically diffed against the genome, and its
        rollback rehearsed. Every output is kept under its own sha256.</li>
      <li><strong>Review.</strong> You see the source diff, the semantic diff
        and the evidence table together, pinned to one candidate commit.
        The verdict is yours, from the terminal or from iris.</li>
      <li><strong>Apply, express, observe.</strong> Approval applies exactly
        that commit. The host rebuilds and restarts the program, watches it
        for a window, and the change is retained or rolled back.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="muted">
      Every step is an event in a hash-chained Journal. When the process and
      the repository disagree about what happened, the Journal is the
      authority, and <code>hale dna history</code> walks it.
    </p>

    <h2>A session</h2>
    <p>
      Attach it to a program that already passes its checks, start it, ask
      for something. This is a small chat server; the editor here runs on
      scripted models, so the transcript is reproducible — the same commands
      and the same Journal with a hosted model behind
      <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="code"><div class="code-head"><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="code-title">terminal</span></div><pre><code>{session}</code></pre></div>
    <p>
      Seconds later there is a pending Review. What you decide on:
    </p>
    <div class="code"><div class="code-head"><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="code-title">terminal</span></div><pre><code>{review}</code></pre></div>
    <p>
      Approval is the apply. The host does the rest and reports back:
    </p>
    <div class="code"><div class="code-head"><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="code-title">terminal</span></div><pre><code>{after}</code></pre></div>
    <p>
      And the whole lineage of that one change, receipts included, is one
      listing:
    </p>
    <div class="code"><div class="code-head"><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="code-title">terminal</span></div><pre><code>{history}</code></pre></div>

    <h2>What keeps it honest</h2>
    <p>
      The rules are not settings. They are Hale source the compiler reads.
      <code>hale dna init</code> generates a constitution into your
      application's own seed, and <code>hale check</code> evaluates it over
      the assembly you actually constructed:
    </p>
    <div class="code"><div class="code-head"><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="code-title">dna_constitution.hl</span></div><pre><code>{law}</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The editor cannot reach git, the network, a deployment, or
        the organism's memory.</strong> It holds none of them, and a wiring
        that hands it one fails the build with a witness path.</li>
      <li><strong>Nothing applies except through the assembly's gate, after
        a settled Review.</strong> A verdict names a candidate commit; a
        candidate that moved after you looked is refused by digest.</li>
      <li><strong>An apply is journaled before it is dispatched.</strong> A
        crashed and retried apply reads its own record and never commits
        twice. A rollback is a reset to a base the Journal recorded.</li>
      <li><strong>Credentials stay sealed.</strong> An API key is read from
        the environment into a locus nothing can read back from, and every
        model call leaves evidence — digests and cost, never the prompt.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What it is not</h2>
    <p>
      DNA is not an agent editing your program while you sleep. The design
      was gated on a falsification test: reviewers deciding from a
      generated semantic diff alone, the git diff alone, and both. The
      semantic view decided the law and structure cases well and could not
      see handler behaviour at all — a <code>+1</code> becoming
      <code>+100</code> is invisible to it. So the Review always shows the
      source beside the model, every change in this phase blocks on a
      human, and the autonomy a project grants its organism is a boundary
      written in the law, with hard edges (touching law, widening effects,
      crossing ownership) that no amount of confidence cancels.
    </p>

    <h2>Get started</h2>
    <div class="install" style="margin-bottom:0.6rem;">
      <span class="sigil">$</span>
      <span class="cmd">hale dna init . &amp;&amp; hale dna run</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      Then read the Book's DNA section: <a href="/docs/dna">what it is</a>,
      {' '}<a href="/docs/dna/attach">attaching it</a>,
      {' '}<a href="/docs/dna/walkthrough">a change end to end</a>,
      {' '}<a href="/docs/dna/review">reviewing</a>,
      {' '}<a href="/docs/dna/autonomy">autonomy and policy</a>, and the
      {' '}<a href="/docs/dna/reference">reference</a>. The design it
      implements is the
      {' '}<a href="https://github.com/hale-lang/hale/issues/521">Hale DNA RFC</a>;
      the friction it has logged against the language is in the repository
      beside it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <style>
    .loop li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
    .code pre { margin: 0; overflow-x: auto; }
    .code code { font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.45; }
  </style>
</Base>
